WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.280
As always, Super Bowl LVII was a huge deal&nbsp;
for commercial lovers, but there were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.540
few big ads during the Big Game that were&nbsp;
more than a little confusing. Here's why.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:15.000
The Super Bowl is known for wild TV team-ups&nbsp;
and pop culture collabs, like Ozzy Osbourne and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.420
Justin Bieber, or Dr. Evil and General Motors. In&nbsp;
2023, there's "The Busch Guy" and Sarah McLachlan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.760
John Cena's team-up with Experian, and Budweiser&nbsp;
betting big on a Kevin Bacon-voiced spot.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.240
But instead of just bringing together&nbsp;
celebrities and brands like the NFL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.220
championship game always does, Super Bowl&nbsp;
LVII kicked off a new trend that will see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.301
the companies themselves linking up&nbsp;
for the widely-watched sports event.

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:40.691
"It's Miller Time."

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.300
A February 9, 2023 report from The&nbsp;
Hollywood Reporter described how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.960
brands are pooling their resources and&nbsp;
star power this year, with marketers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.160
being on the prowl more than ever now for&nbsp;
brand collabs as a way to mitigate costs.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:58.440
Warner Bros. Discovery CEO David Zaslav&nbsp;
called the advertising market "very weak"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.360
back in November 2022, and it's reportedly even&nbsp;
worse these days than it was during the peak of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.700
the COVID-19 pandemic. As a result, TV viewers saw&nbsp;
companies partnering up for commercials this year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.757
— potentially muddying the waters when it comes to&nbsp;
what viewers will actually take away from the ad.

00:01:13.757 --> 00:01:15.233
"Brad was so much better for you.&nbsp;
You should have gone with Brad.

00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:16.860
"Yeah, Brad."

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:17.700
"Are you serious?"

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.140
For example: Michelob ULTRA's Super Bowl&nbsp;
collaboration with Netflix for its new golf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.300
documentary series "Full Swing." The first episode&nbsp;
actually "premieres" via a QR code on the can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.640
as Ricardo Marques, vice president of marketing&nbsp;
for Michelob ULTRA, explained in a statement:

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.060
"If you scan our Michelob Ultra&nbsp;
can, you can access Episode One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.020
of the show exclusively for a period&nbsp;
of 24 hours [...] Thematically this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.080
beautifully aligned with our entire&nbsp;
campaign, and also gives something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.460
of added value to our audience, which is&nbsp;
exclusive access to this amazing show."

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.780
In addition to Michelob, Netflix also&nbsp;
teamed up with GM this year for a Will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.980
Ferrell-led commercial that sees him portraying&nbsp;
popular characters like Dustin Henderson from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.600
"Stranger Things" and a competitor on "Squid&nbsp;
Game" — in an ad about … electric vehicles?

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.780
In similar fashion, brands&nbsp;
like Coors and DraftKings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.680
Marvel and Heineken, Michelob and&nbsp;
Instacart, and a number of others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.460
also decided to work together on Super Bowl&nbsp;
ads this year, which, according to marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.600
execs who spoke to The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;
under the condition of anonymity, is all due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.400
to the market climate. Northwestern University&nbsp;
marketing professor Tim Calkins told the outlet,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.320
"The efficiency argument is pretty compelling,&nbsp;
because doing a Super Bowl ad campaign is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.100
enormously expensive. It's not just the ad&nbsp;
time, it's everything that goes around it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.700
all the other events, all the other&nbsp;
activities, that becomes wildly expensive."

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.440
Netflix didn't do any stand-alone ads this&nbsp;
year during the big game, which is a first for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.180
the streaming giant. According to The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter, GM gave Netflix full and total control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.800
over creating their commercial and simply provided&nbsp;
the vehicles for the entertainment company to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.534
use however they saw fit. Deborah Wahl, chief&nbsp;
marketing officer at GM, said in a statement,

00:02:51.534 --> 00:02:55.325
"Netflix is a great partner because of the&nbsp;
company's compelling storytelling, commitment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.325 --> 00:02:59.880
to sustainability and track record of sparking&nbsp;
conversations that shape cultural trends."

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.540
Marketing executives who spoke to The&nbsp;
Hollywood Reporter insist that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.500
collabs are multi-million-dollar efforts&nbsp;
by the companies to save themselves from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.700
spending too much on an ad that could&nbsp;
possibly miss the mark. Media consultant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:16.699
Brad Adgate says it was a milestone Big&nbsp;
Game, advertising-wise, telling the outlet,

00:03:16.755 --> 00:03:21.075
"Years from now, we'll look back on this and&nbsp;
say this was kind of a watershed moment."

